<h1>Quick Sort</h1>
<p>
    Quick Sort begins by selecting an element from the array, 
    usually the left-most, right-most, or middle element. 
    This element is usually called the pivot, and is represented by the blue bar in the visualization.
    Then we swap the pivot into the first position in the array.
    <br>
    <br>
    Then Quick Sort sets two variables, which we will call <b>A</b> and <b>B</b>, 
    as the second and last indices of the array respectively. 
    <b>A</b> and <b>B</b> are represented by the green and red bars in the visualization.
    It will then loop <b>A</b> up through the array until <b>A</b> finds an element
    that is larger than the pivot.
    Once it does, it will begin to loop <b>B</b> back through the array
    until <b>B</b> finds an element that is smaller than the pivot.
    Finally, it will swap the elements at <b>A</b> and <b>B</b>
    then continue to loop <b>A</b> through the array.
    It will repeat this process until <b>A</b> and <b>B</b> meet, 
    at which point it will swap the pivot and the element behind <b>A</b> and <b>B</b>.
    <br>
    <br>
    At this point the array is split into three sections. 
    Everything smaller than the pivot is to the left,
    everything bigger than it is to the right,
    and the pivot itself is in the center.
    Next, Quick Sort repeats the entire process from before for both the left and right sections.
    It will continue to do this until it reaches a section that is empty or only has one element.
    <br>
    <br>
    At best, Quick Sort splits the array in half each time. 
    On average it splits the array into two halves, two sections of 1/3 and 2/3, or somewhere in between.
    In the worst case it splits the array into just two sections every repetition, the pivot and everything else.
    If you want to see the worst case, try running Quick Sort on an ascending or descending array!
    <br>
    <br>
    Although Quick Sort does not make a copy of the array, 
    it uses extra data based on how many times it splits the array, 
    making it use more memory for larger arrays.
    <br>
    <br>
    Despite Quick Sort's poorly performing worst case, 
    Quick Sort is widely used do to its good average Time Complexity.
</p>

