<h2>Median of Three</h2>
<p>
    Median of Three is a common variant for QuickSort that changes how the pivot is selected.
    <br>
    While regular Quick Sort often selects the first, 
    last, or middle element as the pivot,
    Median of Three starts by sorting the first, last, and middle elements of the current section.
    After sorting them it picks the new middle element as the pivot and moves it to just
    in front of the first element.
    <br>
    <br>
    This process helps avoid the worst case of Quick Sort, 
    where the pivot is the smallest or largest element in that section.
    Try comparing regular Quick Sort and Median of Three on an ascending or descending array to see the difference!
    This gives Median of Three a better average Time Complexity than regular Quick Sort,
    although its best and worst case Time Complexities are the same.
    <br>
    <br>
    This visualization also lets you choose the smallest or largest of the three elements as the pivot, 
    although these elments would not be chosen in real life.
</p>

<h2>Dual Pivot Quick Sort</h2>
<p>
    Dual Pivot Quick Sort is a variant of Quick Sort that uses two pivots instead of one.
    <br>
    It starts by selecting two pivots, which are represented by the two blue bars in the visualization.
    Usually these pivots are the first and last elements of the current section, 
    and they are swapped so that the smaller pivot is in front of the larger one.
    <br>
    <br>
    Then, it uses three variables, which we will call <b>A</b>, <b>C</b>, and <b>B</b>.
</p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <b>A</b>, which is represented by the left-most, green bar in the visualization,
            starts at the second element of the current section, just in front of the smaller pivot.
            The section in between the smaller pivot and <b>A</b> represents all of the elements that are smaller than the smaller pivot.
        </li>
        <li>
            <b>C</b>, which is represented by the right-most, green bar in the visualization,
            starts at the second-to-last element of the current section, just behind the larger pivot.
            The section in between <b>B</b> and the larger pivot represents all of the elements that are larger than the larger pivot.
        </li>
        <li>
            <b>B</b>, which is represented by the red bar in the visualization, starts at the same place as <b>A</b>.
            It then loops through the array. 
            When it finds an element that is smaller than the smaller pivot, 
            it swaps that element with the element at <b>A</b> and moves <b>A</b> forward.
            When it finds an element that is larger than the larger pivot, 
            it swaps that element with the element at <b>C</b> and moves <b>C</b> backward.
            <b>B</b> stops once it reaches <b>C</b>.
        </li>
    </ul>

<p>
    Once <b>B</b> reaches <b>C</b>, 
    the smaller pivot is swapped with the element just behind <b>A</b>, 
    and the larger pivot is swapped with the element just in front of <b>C</b>.
    <br>
    <br>
    At this point the array is split into three sections. 
    Dual Pivot Quick Sort then repeats the process for the each of the three sections,
    similar to regular Quick Sort.
    <br>
    <br>
    Dual Pivot Quick Sort is used in Java's built in sorting method,
    which additionally uses Insertion Sort for smaller sections of the array (usually ~47 elements or less).
</p>

